def s(k,t): S[k]=t
# ---- 13.1
s('1.s1','This chapter is a compendium of the meanings stated in the two preceding sets of six chapters.')
s('1.s2','To elaborate:')
s('1.s3','In the first six chapters, the means of knowledge (jñāna-sādhana) was stated at length up to the end of the sixth chapter, beginning with “the Vedas have the three guṇas as their domain” (Gītā 2.46)<sup>3</sup> and so on; and there, in the second chapter, with “never indeed was I not” (Gītā 2.12)<sup>4</sup> and so on, the nature of the jīva was stated through its being beginningless, eternal, and so on; and in the second six chapters the nature of the Lord was stated; and also, in the seventh [chapter], with “earth, water…” (Gītā 7.4)<sup>5</sup> and so on, the dwelling-place of the Lord, termed “kṣetra,” was stated. All of that, stated in scattered fashion, is condensed — to make it easy for the intellect to take in — and shown in this chapter by way of a question: “prakṛtim” etc.')
s('1.s4','“Puruṣa” is the jīva.')
s('1.s5','“Jñāna” is the means of knowledge. ॥ 1 ॥')
# ---- 13.2
s('2.s1','Objection: since only knowledge of the Lord’s own nature is the cause of liberation, as was said before, stating that nature [alone] would be proper.')
s('2.s2','“Why, then, is the nature of the kṣetra stated here, with ‘mahābhūtāni’ etc.<sup>2</sup>?” — if it be so asked: no [there is no fault].')
s('2.s3','Because it is of use for knowledge of the Lord as qualified by being the kṣetrajña, the mention of the kṣetra is [made]; so, first indicating the kṣetra, He states the Lord’s kṣetrajña-hood: “idam” etc.')
s('2.s4','“O Kaunteya.”')
s('2.s5','“This body” — the unmanifest, the mahat, ahaṅkāra and the rest.')
s('2.s6','Because it is the Lord’s dwelling-place, by the derivation “kṣīyate — it is dwelt in here by the Lord (sthīyate ’tra bhagavatā),” it is called (abhidhīyate) “kṣetra.”')
s('2.s7','“This” — [the body] beginning with the unmanifest, termed “kṣetra.”')
s('2.s8','“Who knows (vetti)” — [i.e.] understands.')
s('2.s9','“Him.”')
s('2.s10','“Tadvidaḥ” — those who know the kṣetra.')
s('2.s11','“They call [him] ‘kṣetrajña’” — that is the meaning.')
s('2.s12','And the reason the word “śarīra” denotes the unmanifest etc. is that [it] is a cause of harm and the like to the jīva by way of separation from its own modifications, the body and so on.')
s('2.s13','[It comes] from the root “śar śṛ” in the sense of harming — “śar”; and, because it is to be impelled by the Lord, “īra” as well.')
s('2.s14','From the root “īr” in the sense of impelling.')
s('2.s15','“Śar” and that “īra” — thus “śarīra” by derivation — so it should be understood. (Bhāva: “śarīra” = śar, that which brings harm to the jīva + īra, that which is impelled by the Lord.)')
s('2.s16','Thereby is set aside the doubt: given “saṅghātaś cetanā” [13.7], how can the body, stated by the word “saṅghāta” as only one part among the modifications of the kṣetra, be said to be the whole kṣetra? ॥ 2 ॥')
# ---- 13.3
s('3.s1','“Since the jīva too has some knowledge of the kṣetra, is he then the kṣetrajña?” — to this He says: “kṣetrajñaṃ ca” etc.')
s('3.s2','“In all kṣetras” — in the unmanifest and the rest.')
s('3.s3','“But know Me as the kṣetrajña.”')
s('3.s4','Not the jīva.')
s('3.s5','The knowledge of kṣetra and kṣetrajña — that is “[the] knowledge.”')
s('3.s6','“Mama samyagjñānaṃ mataṃ” — “it is considered by Me to be right knowledge” — that is the meaning.')
s('3.s7','Here, the expression “in all kṣetras” is for the purpose of indicating that the unmanifest etc., denoted by the word “śarīra” in “idaṃ śarīram,” are called by the word “kṣetra” through the derivation “kṣīyate ’tra īśvareṇa” (it is dwelt in here by the Lord). ॥ 3 ॥')
# ---- 13.4
s('4.s1','Wishing to state the nature of the kṣetra and the rest, He announces: “tat kṣetram” etc.')
s('4.s2','“That kṣetra” — that which, as the Lord’s dwelling-place, was spoken of by the word “kṣetra.”')
s('4.s3','That unmanifest etc. which, through the body, as a cause of harm to the jīva and as that which is impelled by the Lord, was introduced by the word “śarīra” — “yac ca,” of what nature it is; “yādṛk,” of what kind; “yadvikāri,” with what modification.')
s('4.s4','[The word is formed by adding] the suffix matup to a karmadhāraya [compound].')
s('4.s5','[It means] endowed with which modification.')
s('4.s6','“Yataḥ” — from the person endowed with the properties conducive to whatever kind of activity.')
s('4.s7','“Yat” — the present-participle (śatṛ) form of the root “iṇ.”')
s('4.s8','“Gatimat” — moving.')
s('4.s9','That is, “acting.”')
s('4.s10','“Sa ca” — that impeller as well.')
s('4.s11','“Yaḥ” — of what nature.')
s('4.s12','“Yatprabhāvaḥ” — possessed of a capacity other than the properties conducive to whatever kind of activity.')
s('4.s13','“All that.”')
s('4.s14','“Samāsena” — in brief.')
s('4.s15','“From Me.”')
s('4.s16','“Hear” — having heard, make it your firm conviction. ॥ 4 ॥')
# ---- 13.5
s('5.s1','To generate the listeners’ faith, He says that what is being stated is authoritative: “ṛṣibhiḥ” etc.')
s('5.s2','“Tat” is to be supplied.')
s('5.s3','That nature of the kṣetra and the rest which was spoken of with “hear” — that.')
s('5.s4','“Sung by the sages in many ways.”')
s('5.s5','[The root] “gai” is in the sense of sound.')
s('5.s6','The meaning is: “said.”')
s('5.s7','“Vividhaiḥ chandobhiḥ” — by various meters, that is, Vedas.')
s('5.s8','“Gītam” [“sung”] is to be connected with each, throughout.')
s('5.s9','“Hetumadbhiḥ” — furnished with reasoning.')
s('5.s10','“And by the Brahma-sūtra words.”')
s('5.s11','“Hear what has been well ascertained” — this is the construction. ॥ 5 ॥')
# ---- 13.6
s('6.s1','“Kṣetraṃ yac ca yādṛk ca tac chṛṇu” — [the thing] so announced, He states: “mahābhūtāni” etc.')
s('6.s2','The five great elements.')
s('6.s3','The mahat-tattva too is taken by the word “ahaṅkāra.”')
s('6.s4','Buddhi — a particular [function] of the inner instrument.')
s('6.s5','The unmanifest — the pradhāna, made of the three guṇas.')
s('6.s6','“The ten and one senses” — [that is,] the eleven senses, by way of the division into the cognitive senses and the mind.')
s('6.s7','“Indriyagocarāḥ” — the objects of the senses beginning with hearing, sound and the rest, five in number.')
s('6.s8','“This kṣetra.”')
s('6.s9','“In brief.”')
s('6.s10','The construction is: “[the kṣetra] has been described as possessed of such features as being the great elements.” ॥ 6 ॥')
# ---- 13.7
s('7.s1','“Kṣetraṃ yadvikāri” — the kṣetra’s modifications, thus announced, He states: “icchā” etc.')
s('7.s2','“Saṅghāta” is the body.')
s('7.s3','“Cetanā” is the pervasion of the mind.')
s('7.s4','“Dhṛti” is fortitude.')
s('7.s5','“This” — as stated before.')
s('7.s6','“Kṣetra” — endowed with modifications such as desire — [has been] briefly stated: that is the meaning.')
s('7.s7','[Now follow] the presiding deities of the great elements and the other items of the kṣetra.')
s('7.s8','And the presiders over its modifications — desire and the rest — are also mentioned, because they too must necessarily be known.')
s('7.s9','Of ākāśa, the presider is Vighneśa.')
s('7.s10','Of air, the presider is Marīci by name, born of Mukhyavāyu.')
s('7.s11','Of fire, Vahni.')
s('7.s12','Of water, Varuṇa.')
s('7.s13','Of earth, the presiding goddess Dharādevī.')
s('7.s14','Ahaṅkāra: Śiva.')
s('7.s15','Mahat: Brahmā.')
s('7.s16','Buddhi: Umā.')
s('7.s17','The unmanifest: Śrīdevī.')
s('7.s18','Among the senses, the presiders of the mind: Skanda, Indra and Aniruddha.')
s('7.s19','The ear: Candra.')
s('7.s20','The skin: Marut, the son of Vāyu.')
s('7.s21','The eye: Sūrya.')
s('7.s22','The tongue: another form of Varuṇa.')
s('7.s23','The nose: the two Aśvins.')
s('7.s24','Speech: Agni.')
s('7.s25','The hands: the two Maruts, sons of Vāyu.')
s('7.s26','The feet: the two sons of Śacī, entered by Viṣṇu, named Yajña and Śambhu.')
s('7.s27','The organ of excretion: Yama.')
s('7.s28','The organ of generation: Śiva and Manu.')
s('7.s29','Among the objects of the senses — sound, touch, form, taste and smell — the presiders are to be known as the five sons of Śiva.')
s('7.s30','Among the modifications, desire: Śrī and Bhāratī.')
s('7.s31','The presiders of aversion and pain: Kali and Dvāpara.')
s('7.s32','Pleasure: Mukhyavāyu.')
s('7.s33','Fortitude: Sarasvatī and Bhāratī.')
s('7.s34','Consciousness: Śrī.')
s('7.s35','The presiders of the aggregate are other conscious beings; they are to be known [as such].')
s('7.s36','That the presiders too are named “kṣetra” is only because they are the Lord’s dwelling — by the derivation stated earlier.')
s('7.s37','And that the kṣetra has modifications is because, in relation to the mahat and the rest, it stands as a modification in a still higher degree — thus it should be understood. ॥ 7 ॥')
# ---- 13.8
s('8.s1','Having thus stated, among the six announced topics, the three — “yac ca,” “yādṛk ca,” and “yadvikārī” — and wishing to state the remaining two announced topics, “sa ca yaḥ” and “yatprabhāvaś ca,” with “jñeyaṃ yat tat” [13.13] and so on, [He does so] with the thought that these do not become realized in experience through hearing of just any sort; so He states the means of the knowledge of the Lord — who constitutes the two objects of knowledge to be spoken of — by the group of five [verses]: “amānitvam” etc.')
s('8.s2','“Amānitvam” — freedom from self-praise (the absence of praising oneself).')
s('8.s3','Displaying greatness even after knowing one’s own smallness is “dambha.”')
s('8.s4','The absence of that is “adambhitvam.”')
s('8.s5','“Kṣānti” — forbearance.')
s('8.s6','“Ārjavam” — non-contradiction among mind, speech, body and action, in the right path.')
s('8.s7','“Ācāryopāsanam” — service of those who know.')
s('8.s8','“Śaucam” — outer and inner purity.')
s('8.s9','“Purity is declared twofold, external and likewise internal; external is held to be by earth and water, internal by purity of the inner state (bhāva)” — according to this smṛti.')
s('8.s10','“Sthairyam” — not swerving from one’s own dharma through fear and the like.')
s('8.s11','“Ātmavinigraha” — special restraint of the mind. ॥ 8 ॥')
# ---- 13.9
s('9.s1','“Indriyārtheṣu vairāgyam” — absence of attachment to sound and the other [objects].')
s('9.s2','“Anahaṅkāraḥ” — absence of the delusion of pride in qualities of the self that do not exist.')
s('9.s3','Either “the keeping in view of both pain and evil in birth and the rest,” or “the keeping in view of the evil that is of the nature of pain.”')
s('9.s4','Continuous reflection (anusandhāna) — that is the meaning. ॥ 9 ॥')
# ---- 13.10
s('10.s1','Toward son, wife, home and the like, “asakti” is absence of affection.')
s('10.s2','“Anabhiṣvaṅga” is absence of overripe affection.')
s('10.s3','“Iṣṭāniṣṭopapattiṣu” —')
s('10.s4','“Upapatti” is attainment; in that, “constant evenness of mind” is absence of both joy and dejection. ॥ 10 ॥')
# ---- 13.11
s('11.s1','“Ananyayogena” — by [a yoga] unconnected with the independence of Ramā, Brahmā and others; that is, [devotion] that does not have those [as independent] for its object.')
s('11.s2','Hence it is “unswerving.”')
s('11.s3','“Devotion in Me.”')
s('11.s4','“Viviktadeśasevitvam” — dwelling in a pure place free from the press of people. ॥ 11 ॥')
# ---- 13.12
s('12.s1','“Adhyātmajñānanityatvam” — steadiness in knowledge established with reference to the Supreme Self.')
s('12.s2','“Tattvajñānārthadarśanam” —')
s('12.s3','Reflection on the scriptures for the sake of direct knowledge.')
s('12.s4','“This” — the group of twenty dharmas stated by “amānitvam” etc.')
s('12.s5','“Jñānam iti” — by the derivation “jñāyate ’nena” (that by which one knows), [the group] is said to be the means of knowledge.')
s('12.s6','“Ato ’nyathā yat asti tad ajñānam” — whatever exists otherwise than this is ignorance; it is not a means of knowledge — that is the meaning.')
s('12.s7','Or: the word “jñāna,” by the derivation in the sense of the action itself (bhāva), denotes awareness (saṃvit) as well.')
s('12.s8','“Tattvajñānārthasya” — of the object of true knowledge — “darśanam” — the vision of Viṣṇu; that is, direct knowledge (aparokṣa-jñāna).')
s('12.s9','This direct knowledge of the Lord is declared to be “jñāna.”')
s('12.s10','“Ato ’nyathā yat” — whatever is otherwise than this is not knowledge.')
s('12.s11','Here the word “jñāna” denotes both knowledge and its means.')
s('12.s12','And “tattvajñānārthadarśanam” too, as explained, denotes the means of knowledge consisting in scriptural reflection, and also direct knowledge.')
s('12.s13','Therefore, referring to both by the word “etat,” the predication by the word “jñāna” of being a means of knowledge and of being knowledge — through the derivation in both the bhāva and the karaṇa senses — is proper: so it should be understood. ॥ 12 ॥')
