# ---- footnotes: (verse, num) -> english
FN={
(0,1):'“[He] gathers together and distinguishes the previously stated knowledge, object of knowledge, kṣetra and puruṣa, and shows them in this chapter” — Gītā-bhāṣya. “This is a summary of all the meanings” — Gītā-tātparya.',
(1,2):'This verse is not read in some [texts]; but it is known to be accepted by Śrīmad Ācārya (Madhva).',
(1,3):'Gītā (2.46).',
(1,4):'Gītā (2.12).',
(1,5):'Gītā (7.4).',
(2,6):'“The Lord Viṣṇu, the support of Himself, who by His own will is hidden from the jīva’s sight, is called ‘kṣetrajña’ — the Supreme Person of the jīva.” — so too in Bhāgavata-tātparya (5/11/14).',
(2,2):'Gītā (13.6).',
(5,1):'“Chandas” [is used] in the senses of verse, Veda, and unrestrained conduct or desire — so [says] the Medinī [lexicon].',
(5,2):'“Brahma-sūtras are the Śārīraka-sūtras” — Gītā-bhāṣya. “Brahma-sūtras are the statements that point to Brahman” (Prameya-dīpikā). “Brahma [means] the Veda, its meaning, or the Supreme Brahman; the sūtras of that are the Brahma-sūtras — that is, the sūtras meant to determine the nature of the Supreme Brahman, Viṣṇu, who embodies the meaning of the entire Veda.” — Nyāya-sudhā. “Hence the Śārīra is the Supreme Self alone. Both [the jīva and Īśvara] are to be known as ‘śārīra’ — the jīva and the one called Īśvara. Because it concerns Him, who is full of all qualities, the inquiry is called Śārīraka-mīmāṃsā.” — Nyāya-sudhā.',
(7,1):'“The experience of bliss is held to be the best of all experiences. To be endowed with it — as with mildness (saumya) — is indeed superior among the guṇas. [The reason:] even a whole host of qualities such as devotion becomes fruitless when it is thought of as a cause of pain, whereas for one who is delighted it bears fruit. Therefore the state of bliss alone is declared the best among the guṇas. Its presiding deity is Brahmā alone — [he presides] also over devotion, knowledge and the like; likewise the goddess who presides over faith (śraddhā) is Sarasvatī. Of the remaining guṇas, the presiding deities are the gods remembered as following him. Brahmā is the lord of the guṇas; therefore the four-faced one alone [stands first among them]. The ‘autpattika-guṇa’ (innate quality) is the name for the one fitness for attaining the good. Its presiding deity is Prāṇa, who indeed excels all in guṇas. The meaning is this: the innate guṇa abides in each possessor of guṇas, like a thread (sūtra) running through them. That fitness for guṇas which abides in all possessors of guṇas — Vāyu alone is its presiding deity; therefore he excels all possessors of guṇas.” — so in the Prabhañjana.',
(8,2):'“He who gathers (ācinoti) right conduct, makes others adopt right conduct, and himself practises it — him they call ācārya” — so in the Bhārata. Principally, the ācārya is Śrīmad Madhvācārya alone — because of the statement “the ācāryas are the glorious Ācārya.”',
(10,1):'“Association with good men and likewise with women brings no fault; applied appropriately it is only for good; but toward the wicked and harmful creatures it makes for fault” — so in the Vārāha; Bhāgavata-tātparya (3/32/41). “[Relatives such as] father and the like are bound by transient relation; Hari alone is the eternally-related” — and so on; Bhāgavata-tātparya (6/16/9). “One should never become excessively attached or entangled with anyone anywhere; he who does so comes to grief, like the pitiable-minded dove” — Bhāgavata (11/7/52). “All accumulations end in loss, all risings in a fall, all unions in separation, and life in death; therefore toward sons, wives, friends and wealth one should not be over-attached, for separation from them is certain” — Vālmīki-Rāmāyaṇa. “True manifestation of one’s nature, and absence of [further] birth — [these come] without effort, thereafter growth; the best is the company of the good” — so in the Vāyu-proktam; Bhāgavata-tātparya (1/18/13). “The seeker who needs many things attaches himself somewhat even to the good, when it is impossible to carry on [otherwise]. One who has done what is to be done may give up attachment — always toward gurus, gods and so on. One does not become an [indiscriminate] attacher, nor does one get happiness without that attachment; therefore attachment to gurus, gods and the like is beginningless and endless; elsewhere it depends on need — such is the determination of attachment” — so in the Bhaviṣya-parva; Bhāgavata-tātparya (11/3/24). “One should never live with the wicked; as for the many good, association with them gives liberation” — so in the Ṣāḍguṇya; Bhāgavata-tātparya (11/9/10).',
}
